BT /F1 10 Tf 50 800 Td 13 TL (University of Brackenford, School of Economics) Tj T* (BSc Economics, final-year dissertation \(EC3990\), 2025-26) Tj T* (Distance to campus and the decision to go to university: evidence from the Brackenford) Tj T* (Household Panel) Tj T* (Candidate number: B204117. Supervisor: Dr H. Lowther. Word count: 9,870.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Extract: Chapter 1 \(Question\), Chapter 3 \(Data\), Chapter 4 \(Method\), Chapter 5 \(Results\) and) Tj T* (Chapter 6 \(Conclusion\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (1. The question) Tj T* () Tj T* (Widening participation policy in England has mostly focused on fees, maintenance support and) Tj T* (contextual offers. Much less attention has gone to geography. A young person who lives a short) Tj T* (bus ride from a university can study while living at home, keep a part-time job and avoid the) Tj T* (cost of halls. A young person who lives an hour or more away faces a very different decision.) Tj T* (This dissertation asks a narrow question: does living further from the nearest university) Tj T* (campus lower the probability that a young person in England enters higher education by age 19,) Tj T* (once family background and school results are held constant? A second question is whether the) Tj T* (effect is larger for pupils who were eligible for free school meals.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The answer matters for where new campuses and satellite sites are placed. If distance has a) Tj T* (real effect on participation, and that effect falls hardest on poorer pupils, then the location) Tj T* (of provision is itself a widening participation tool.) Tj T* () Tj T* (3. Data) Tj T* () Tj T* (The data come from the Brackenford Household Panel, a fictional longitudinal survey used for) Tj T* (teaching in the School of Economics. The sample is every panel member who turned 19 between) Tj T* (2016 and 2022 and who lived in England at age 16. After dropping 337 cases with missing school) Tj T* (results or postcodes, the analysis sample has 4,812 young people living in 312 local areas.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The outcome is a binary indicator equal to 1 if the young person had started a degree course by) Tj T* (age 19. Overall, 41.6% of the sample had done so. The key explanatory variable is the) Tj T* (straight-line distance in kilometres from the home postcode at age 16 to the nearest university) Tj T* (campus, measured in units of 10 km. Mean distance is 18.4 km and the median is 12.7 km; 9% of) Tj T* (the sample live more than 50 km from a campus.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Controls are: Key Stage 4 attainment \(average point score\), free school meals eligibility at) Tj T* (age 16 \(23.5% of the sample\), parental education \(whether either parent has a degree\),) Tj T* (household income band, sex, ethnicity and the year the young person turned 19.) Tj T* () Tj T* (4. Method) Tj T* () Tj T* (The main model is a linear probability model estimated by ordinary least squares, with standard) Tj T* (errors clustered at the local area level. I estimate three specifications. Model 1 includes) Tj T* (distance only. Model 2 adds the individual and family controls. Model 3 adds local authority) Tj T* (fixed effects, so that distance is identified only from differences within the same local) Tj T* (authority. This guards against the worry that areas far from universities differ in other ways,) Tj T* (such as the local labour market for school leavers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (I chose a linear probability model over a probit because the coefficients can be read directly) Tj T* (as percentage point changes and because the predicted probabilities for 98% of the sample lie) Tj T* (between 0 and 1. As a robustness check I also estimate probit models and report average) Tj T* (marginal effects, which are within 0.002 of the linear estimates in every specification.) Tj T* () Tj T* (To test the second question, Model 4 interacts distance with free school meals eligibility.) Tj T* () Tj T* (5. Results) Tj T* ET
