BT /F1 10 Tf 50 800 Td 13 TL () Tj T* (Table 5.1: Effect of distance to the nearest campus \(per 10 km\) on entering higher education by) Tj T* (age 19) Tj T* () Tj T* (                                 Model 1     Model 2     Model 3     Model 4) Tj T* (Distance \(per 10 km\)             -0.031      -0.024      -0.019      -0.017) Tj T* (                                 \(0.009\)     \(0.008\)     \(0.010\)     \(0.009\)) Tj T* (Distance x FSM eligible                                              -0.024) Tj T* (                                                                     \(0.011\)) Tj T* (Controls                         No          Yes         Yes         Yes) Tj T* (Local authority fixed effects    No          No          Yes         Yes) Tj T* (Observations                     4,812       4,812       4,812       4,812) Tj T* (R-squared                        0.02        0.21        0.26        0.26) Tj T* () Tj T* (Standard errors clustered by local area in brackets.) Tj T* () Tj T* (In Model 1, each extra 10 km between home and the nearest campus is associated with a 3.1) Tj T* (percentage point lower probability of entering higher education. Adding controls reduces this) Tj T* (to 2.4 percentage points, which shows that part of the raw gap reflects family background and) Tj T* (attainment. With local authority fixed effects the estimate falls to 1.9 percentage points and) Tj T* (is significant at the 10% level only.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Model 4 shows the effect is concentrated among poorer pupils. For pupils not eligible for free) Tj T* (school meals, an extra 10 km lowers the probability by 1.7 percentage points. For eligible) Tj T* (pupils the combined effect is 4.1 percentage points \(-0.017 plus -0.024\), more than twice as) Tj T* (large. Put another way, for a free school meals pupil living 30 km from a campus rather than) Tj T* (next to it, the predicted probability of entering higher education is about 12 percentage) Tj T* (points lower.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Attainment remains by far the strongest predictor. A one standard deviation increase in the Key) Tj T* (Stage 4 average point score raises the probability by 17.8 percentage points in Model 3.) Tj T* () Tj T* (6. Conclusion) Tj T* () Tj T* (Distance to the nearest campus has a modest but real association with whether young people in) Tj T* (England go to university, and the association is more than twice as strong for pupils eligible) Tj T* (for free school meals. The estimates shrink as better controls are added, so I do not claim) Tj T* (that they are fully causal: families may choose where to live partly on the basis of education.) Tj T* (Even so, the pattern is consistent with distance acting as a cost that bites hardest on poorer) Tj T* (households.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The main policy implication is that satellite campuses, part-time routes and travel support) Tj T* (could be aimed at areas that are both far from a university and have high rates of free school) Tj T* (meals eligibility. A natural next step would be to use the opening of new campuses as a natural) Tj T* (experiment.) Tj T* () Tj T* ET
